{
  "abstract": "We study Collatz trajectories selected to remain above their starting value until a prescribed number of odd-even run pairs has been confirmed, with an additional constraint specifying their exact duration. A two-parameter change of measure matches the duration and centers the block height. The tilted parity bits form a two-state Markov chain. We adapt the complete count clock of Weaver and Wren to this chain by retaining the last bit in the ternary seed estimate and using a soft band for late crossings. A nonnegative stopped product bounds the number of good Fourier phases. The exact-duration conditioning costs only a polynomial prefix factor, which the superpolynomial decay absorbs. This gives relative counts and marked-record transport for actual positive integer sources when the prescribed duration exceeds the source bit length by a small multiple of $(\\log M)^2/\\log\\log M$. A separate clipped tilt and uniform two-step estimates reach the shortest feasible durations for this integer comparison. On compact interior duration ranges, a marked fluctuation identity gives a uniform local asymptotic with an entropy exponent, an $n^{-2}$ factor and a nonconstant periodic phase factor. Bounded exact controls check the clock and likelihood interfaces, including variants that discard the last bit or use the wrong reverse reward. The estimate concerns selected ensembles. The full ordered histories and the unresolved central uncapped tail require different arguments.",
